00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:15.731
And he is seeking.


00:21:16.023 --> 00:21:18.109
He is on a quest, Lord Jesus.


00:21:18.401 --> 00:21:20.318
I pray that you will
just give him the answers


00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:21.861
that he's looking for, Lord.


00:21:22.153 --> 00:21:24.155
We pray your favor upon Dylan.


00:21:24.447 --> 00:21:27.158
We pray that salvation,
healing, and deliverance


00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:30.786
will come to Dylan's
body, soul, and spirit.


00:21:31.078 --> 00:21:34.373
And to his friend, also,
Lord, we just pray for her.


00:21:34.665 --> 00:21:37.667
We pray that as they
travel together, that Lord,


00:21:37.959 --> 00:21:40.210
you would put a hedge of
protection around them


00:21:40.502 --> 00:21:44.547
and that, Lord, your spirit
will fall upon them like it did


00:21:44.839 --> 00:21:47.216
when you fell upon.


00:21:47.508 --> 00:21:50.303
And we just bleed your blood,
Jesus, over their lives,


00:21:50.594 --> 00:21:52.387
over their hearts,
and their minds,


00:21:52.679 --> 00:21:54.723
and their emotions,
Lord, and we say


00:21:55.015 --> 00:21:56.308
thank you, Lord,
for the opportunity


00:21:56.599 --> 00:21:59.226
to pray and bless
them, in Jesus' name.


00:22:03.397 --> 00:22:07.149
- I
- typically never make a sign.


00:22:07.441 --> 00:22:11.194
But I figured, why not?


00:22:11.486 --> 00:22:15.698
A simple sign manifested into
encountering this man, George.


00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:17.033
How do you like
the United States?


00:22:17.325 --> 00:22:18.909
- You like it?
- Yeah.


00:22:19.201 --> 00:22:21.370
He
offered me the $50 bonus


00:22:21.662 --> 00:22:25.582
to tour a luxury timeshare
in North Beach, Miami.


00:22:25.874 --> 00:22:28.709
Guys, um, I need you to
come over here for a minute--


00:22:29.001 --> 00:22:30.252
just initial this.


00:22:30.544 --> 00:22:31.669
It
required a bit of paperwork


00:22:31.961 --> 00:22:36.466
and fibbing as far as my
occupation and salary.


00:22:36.758 --> 00:22:37.800
So what?


00:22:38.092 --> 00:22:40.844
I could use $50 to
help myself and to help


00:22:41.136 --> 00:22:44.890
this man make a few dollars
off of referring me.


00:22:45.182 --> 00:22:46.641
Hey, you got to wash
your hands, all right?


00:22:46.932 --> 00:22:47.558
Come on.


00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:49.602
There's your ID.


00:23:04.489 --> 00:23:05.948
OK?


00:23:06.240 --> 00:23:09.077
You've got to finish the tour
in order to get the gift.


00:23:09.369 --> 00:23:11.078
So an hour, hour
and a half, they're


00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:13.663
going to have snacks up there.


00:23:13.955 --> 00:23:15.707
They're going to tell you
about a vacation club,


00:23:15.999 --> 00:23:17.459
and you know, if
you like it, buy it.


00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:19.751
If you don't-- if you don't make
spur of the moment decisions,


00:23:20.043 --> 00:23:22.045
whatever it is that you do
or don't do, it's up to you.


00:23:22.337 --> 00:23:23.922
You don't have to buy
anything for the gift.


00:23:24.214 --> 00:23:25.757
OK?


00:23:26.049 --> 00:23:27.007
Thank you, again.


00:23:27.299 --> 00:23:28.425
I'll be here when
you get done, OK?


00:23:28.717 --> 00:23:30.219
I'll be here waiting to pay you.


00:23:30.511 --> 00:23:31.762
OK.


00:23:32.054 --> 00:23:36.015
Maybe
about a 25 minute van ride.


00:23:36.307 --> 00:23:39.435
And you end up at this
beautiful facility.


00:23:39.727 --> 00:23:43.604
Sure, I'll pretend to be a
hot shot, wealthy young guy,


00:23:43.896 --> 00:23:46.732
covered in dirt, stinky.


00:24:17.174 --> 00:24:20.094
The pressure of the
older one is always good.


00:24:27.267 --> 00:24:28.726
OK, cool.


00:24:29.018 --> 00:24:30.978
I told
her I was a master carpenter.


00:24:31.269 --> 00:24:33.104
That was my way of
explaining my hands


00:24:33.396 --> 00:24:36.607
being all dirty and stained.


00:24:36.899 --> 00:24:40.068
Right on.


00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:47.199
Yeah?


00:24:47.491 --> 00:24:49.326
Oh, that's good.


00:25:03.546 --> 00:25:04.964
The $50 Dylan


00:25:05.256 --> 00:25:09.134
earned last for a few days,
maybe beyond the few days


00:25:09.426 --> 00:25:11.094
I had with him on the streets.


00:25:11.386 --> 00:25:14.806
But he decided to go to
a restaurant for dinner.


00:25:15.098 --> 00:25:18.601
And the rest of the
money he gave away.


00:25:18.893 --> 00:25:21.769
After a few minutes,
not surprisingly,


00:25:22.061 --> 00:25:23.771
Dylan became friends
with the people


00:25:24.063 --> 00:25:27.441
sitting at the next table.


00:25:27.733 --> 00:25:28.442
What's your name?


00:25:28.734 --> 00:25:29.568
I'm Dylan.


00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:30.527
Dylan? nice to meet you.
Uri.


00:25:30.819 --> 00:25:31.403
Uri?


00:25:31.695 --> 00:25:32.404
Uri, yeah.


00:25:37.575 --> 00:25:38.367
That's my grandma.


00:25:38.659 --> 00:25:39.827
That's your grandma?


00:25:40.119 --> 00:25:41.328
My mom.


00:25:41.620 --> 00:25:42.829
This is my mom.


00:25:51.461 --> 00:25:53.171
Nice to meet you all.


00:25:53.463 --> 00:25:54.631
They gave Dylan $20


00:25:54.923 --> 00:25:56.466
and their contact information,


00:26:01.094 --> 00:26:02.221
I promise.


00:26:09.059 --> 00:26:09.851
I hope not.


00:26:10.143 --> 00:26:10.936
But--


00:26:11.228 --> 00:26:13.437
Yes.


00:26:13.729 --> 00:26:14.313
All right.


00:26:14.605 --> 00:26:17.316
All right.


00:26:17.608 --> 00:26:18.317
I love it.


00:26:18.609 --> 00:26:19.318
I love it.


00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:20.818
Absolutely.


00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:22.278
You guys have
a beautiful night.


00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:23.279
Thank you.
You too.


00:26:23.571 --> 00:26:24.197
Bye.


00:27:20.953 --> 00:27:22.413
- I
- was worried that we would


00:27:22.704 --> 00:27:25.163
be kicked out of this park.


00:27:25.455 --> 00:27:30.294
Sure enough, I got
woken up by the police.


00:27:30.586 --> 00:27:32.253
Fuck!


00:27:32.545 --> 00:27:33.879
You can cavity search me.


00:29:16.048 --> 00:29:17.591
Before we headed north,


00:29:17.883 --> 00:29:20.344
I went to a bathroom in the
bagel shop to clean myself.


00:29:23.846 --> 00:29:25.639
It took me so long
in the bathroom


00:29:25.931 --> 00:29:29.267
that the staff noticed
and questioned me.


00:29:29.559 --> 00:29:33.187
I told them about Dylan
and that I was filming him.


00:29:33.479 --> 00:29:38.650
Dylan, some
bagels for us on the road.


00:29:38.942 --> 00:29:41.778
Do you want to come in
and say thank you to them?


00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:43.405
I don't know why
he's in that situation,


00:29:43.697 --> 00:29:45.073
but for whatever
reason, he's there.


00:29:45.365 --> 00:29:47.282
So why not help?


00:29:47.574 --> 00:29:48.784
How much
money did you give him?


00:29:49.076 --> 00:29:50.284
Just $5. $5.


00:29:50.576 --> 00:29:52.787
Then like a half pack of smokes.


00:29:53.079 --> 00:29:54.287
Then we give him some
bagels and stuff.


00:29:54.579 --> 00:29:56.623
That was the boss's idea there.


00:29:56.915 --> 00:30:00.001
You know, just some bagels
and some cream cheese.


00:30:00.293 --> 00:30:02.545
Heard he was living on
the streets and hungry.


00:30:02.837 --> 00:30:04.004
So you know, why
not help someone?


00:30:06.382 --> 00:30:07.466
Soon after, we


00:30:07.758 --> 00:30:09.009
were back on the road.


00:30:09.301 --> 00:30:11.761
Dylan decided to
sell the bagels.


00:30:12.053 --> 00:30:13.638
No one wanted a bagel.


00:30:13.930 --> 00:30:14.972
But many people
gave him the money.


00:30:18.099 --> 00:30:20.101
What did you tell him?


00:30:20.393 --> 00:30:22.854
I said, you do
you need a bagel for $1?


00:30:23.146 --> 00:30:24.147
I'm trying to hit this bus.


00:30:24.439 --> 00:30:25.064
And he was like, hold on.


00:30:25.356 --> 00:30:26.773
Hold on.


00:30:27.065 --> 00:30:29.234
He went inside and bought some
beer and came back with $1.


00:30:29.526 --> 00:30:31.236
But he didn't ask for a bagel.


00:30:35.406 --> 00:30:37.032
- I
- was annoyed that Dylan wanted


00:30:37.324 --> 00:30:39.075
to get rid of the bagels.


00:30:39.367 --> 00:30:41.286
I felt that he was
ungrateful for the kindness


00:30:41.577 --> 00:30:43.537
people showed him.


00:30:43.829 --> 00:30:46.790
I felt disgusted that I
lost interest in filming


00:30:47.082 --> 00:30:50.376
and gave him the camera.


00:30:50.668 --> 00:30:52.378
When I first met
Dylan, he seemed


00:30:52.670 --> 00:30:54.589
like a symbol for
the free America


00:30:54.881 --> 00:30:57.632
I had always heard about.


00:30:57.924 --> 00:31:01.178
But the longer I spent observing
how he used his freedom,


00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:02.095
my feelings changed.


00:31:05.472 --> 00:31:06.765
Can you spare a dollar?


00:31:32.787 --> 00:31:34.205
I don't eat
that much food.


00:31:34.497 --> 00:31:36.873
I don't want to carry
that around for so long,


00:31:37.165 --> 00:31:40.460
I don't want to have to
take three shits a day.


00:31:40.752 --> 00:31:43.963
I'm not going to get any
money from anybody or food


00:31:44.255 --> 00:31:48.300
if I'm carrying a big
bag of bagels around.


00:31:48.592 --> 00:31:51.386
My objective is
drinking right now.


00:31:51.678 --> 00:31:55.639
And now I have to beg
again, which I never beg.


00:31:55.931 --> 00:31:57.849
I have to beg today
to get some beer--


00:32:03.521 --> 00:32:06.690
so I can think straight enough
to know what's bothering me.


00:32:10.109 --> 00:32:11.319
So you
already knew the answer.


00:32:11.611 --> 00:32:15.073
You already knew it, right?


00:32:15.365 --> 00:32:16.531
I need to drink
and think about it.


00:32:21.494 --> 00:32:24.288
It doesn't involve
you, but I just


00:32:24.579 --> 00:32:28.083
still need to think about it.


00:32:28.375 --> 00:32:30.043
When
he threw away the bagels,


00:32:30.335 --> 00:32:32.628
I couldn't stand it.


00:32:32.920 --> 00:32:34.547
We had an argument.


00:32:34.839 --> 00:32:37.633
I criticized him for taking
advantage of people's kindness.


00:32:42.511 --> 00:32:44.555
But then I thought to myself--


00:32:44.847 --> 00:32:47.641
was I the one who was taking
advantage of him by filming?


00:32:50.685 --> 00:32:55.106
Shortly after this, my
trip with Dylan ended.


00:32:55.398 --> 00:32:56.857
Dylan stayed on the streets.


00:32:57.148 --> 00:32:59.192
And I went back to
my life in New York.


00:33:08.783 --> 00:33:10.451
Everything looked
different after the trip.


00:33:19.834 --> 00:33:21.669
My few weeks on the
streets showed me


00:33:21.961 --> 00:33:27.674
that my everyday worries from
before all seemed pointless.


00:33:27.966 --> 00:33:32.386
I knew that whatever
happened, I could survive.


00:33:32.678 --> 00:33:36.347
I soon became busy making a
film about Chinese human rights.


00:33:39.309 --> 00:33:42.478
The survival skills I learned
on the streets with Dylan


00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:46.189
became useful as I was on
the run across China chased


00:33:46.481 --> 00:33:48.191
by the police.


00:33:48.483 --> 00:33:50.694
I often find myself
comparing my experience


00:33:50.986 --> 00:33:52.987
filming in both countries.


00:33:53.279 --> 00:33:55.447
I felt safer sleeping
on the streets


00:33:55.739 --> 00:33:59.992
than I did traveling in
China on buses and trains.


00:34:00.284 --> 00:34:02.494
Two years quickly went by.


00:34:02.786 --> 00:34:05.748
I often wondered what
would happen to Dylan


00:34:06.040 --> 00:34:07.124
and if I would see him again.


00:34:40.652 --> 00:34:41.527
I started


00:34:41.819 --> 00:34:44.363
doing these around 2013.


00:34:44.655 --> 00:34:46.906
What I would do is
in between calls


00:34:47.198 --> 00:34:49.951
or when I was on call and
driving around in my work


00:34:50.242 --> 00:34:53.537
truck, I would just take the
GoPro to one of the windows


00:34:53.829 --> 00:34:56.831
and just say what
was on my mind.


00:34:57.123 --> 00:34:58.583
It is a beautiful day.


00:35:01.461 --> 00:35:03.504
Got to enjoy those
every chance you get.


00:35:32.195 --> 00:35:37.240
I am headed out to do some
scouting on a target house--


00:35:41.577 --> 00:35:44.371
a little bit.


00:35:44.663 --> 00:35:50.043
And then I got to pick
up some new handcuffs,


00:35:50.334 --> 00:35:58.924
because mine got stuck on
the last guy I arrested.


00:35:59.216 --> 00:36:01.468
And that's really upsetting,
because I've had that pair


00:36:01.760 --> 00:36:03.679
of handcuffs for 24 years--


00:36:03.971 --> 00:36:04.555
an entire career.


00:36:13.646 --> 00:36:15.772
Two
years after I first met Dylan,


00:36:16.064 --> 00:36:18.108
I traveled to Utah
for the first time


00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:22.402
for the Chinese human rights
film I was working on.


00:36:22.694 --> 00:36:24.821
I remembered that
Dylan was from Utah,


00:36:25.113 --> 00:36:27.073
and I was curious to
see if I could learn


00:36:27.365 --> 00:36:31.285
more about where he came from.


00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:47.089
I have
lots of disks where


00:36:47.381 --> 00:36:50.051
I have recorded my thoughts--


00:36:50.342 --> 00:36:53.095
just like a video journal.


00:36:53.386 --> 00:37:01.477
And there's a lot of things that
are special to me, you know,


00:37:01.769 --> 00:37:06.523
that I am glad I
have documented.


00:37:06.815 --> 00:37:09.317
ICAC search warrant
Park City, Utah.


00:37:17.948 --> 00:37:18.741
Police!


00:37:19.033 --> 00:37:19.742
Search warrant!


00:37:29.459 --> 00:37:30.251
Search warrant!
Police!


00:37:30.543 --> 00:37:31.127
Search warrant.


00:37:31.419 --> 00:37:32.045
Police!


00:37:32.337 --> 00:37:33.253
Search warrant!


00:37:33.545 --> 00:37:36.798
I've been a
detective since 2011.


00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:39.801
I investigate sex crimes.


00:37:40.093 --> 00:37:42.385
I'm also assigned to
investigate internet crimes


00:37:42.677 --> 00:37:43.637
against children.


00:37:43.929 --> 00:37:46.056
We'll execute a search warrant.


00:37:46.348 --> 00:37:51.227
High 90% of the time, we find
evidence of child pornography.


00:37:51.519 --> 00:37:53.813
Well, the first
several times, I had


00:37:54.105 --> 00:37:58.233
to review material,
because I can't say, well,


00:37:58.525 --> 00:37:59.817
I think this is child porn.


00:38:00.109 --> 00:38:02.486
I have to review it and
make sure what it is.


00:38:02.778 --> 00:38:04.154
And I have to be
able to describe


00:38:04.446 --> 00:38:07.156
a number of images and videos.


00:38:07.448 --> 00:38:10.451
So the first time I did
have to do those reviews,


00:38:10.743 --> 00:38:12.411
I got some vicarious trauma.


00:38:12.702 --> 00:38:14.454
And I don't cry hardly ever.


00:38:14.746 --> 00:38:20.960
I sat down at my desk one
day and just started to cry.


00:38:21.252 --> 00:38:24.921
This was a way to get
those things off my chest.


00:38:25.213 --> 00:38:26.714
Even though I'm only
talking to myself,


00:38:27.006 --> 00:38:30.634
I couldn't talk to
other people about it.


00:38:30.926 --> 00:38:33.053
Every once in a while,
I'd wake up really early,


00:38:33.345 --> 00:38:34.638
and I just couldn't
go back to bed.


00:38:34.930 --> 00:38:37.765
So I decided just go to work.


00:38:38.057 --> 00:38:41.394
I started the camera
from my driveway.


00:38:41.686 --> 00:38:43.729
And it was super dark, so
you can't see anything.


00:38:44.021 --> 00:38:47.023
But you could hear what
I was talking about.


00:38:47.315 --> 00:38:47.941
I have three kids.


00:38:52.027 --> 00:38:54.070
My youngest is 15.


00:38:54.362 --> 00:38:55.947
He gets straight A's in school--


00:38:56.239 --> 00:38:56.823
very smart.


00:38:59.576 --> 00:39:06.206
My daughter is working on
graduating from college.


00:39:06.498 --> 00:39:09.208
She's so kind hearted and good.


00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:12.503
And my oldest boy--


00:39:12.794 --> 00:39:13.503
he's 23.


00:39:16.714 --> 00:39:20.467
He's been a transient
all around the country.


00:39:31.852 --> 00:39:36.565
No parent wants their child
to be homeless on the street.


00:39:36.857 --> 00:39:38.983
I think when you're a parent,
you want your children


00:39:39.275 --> 00:39:40.650
to be kind like you.


00:39:40.942 --> 00:39:46.198
He looks just like I did when
I was his age or younger.


00:39:46.489 --> 00:39:48.949
We have a lot of similarities
in our voice and the way


00:39:49.241 --> 00:39:50.910
that we really
care about people.


00:39:53.704 --> 00:39:58.416
All three of my children grew
up with the same expectations


00:39:58.708 --> 00:39:59.792
and the same knowledge.


00:40:00.084 --> 00:40:01.877
And they all turned out
so very differently.


00:40:13.470 --> 00:40:17.432
I'm kind
of a religious person.


00:40:17.724 --> 00:40:19.141
Well, I'm actually a
very religious person.


00:40:22.228 --> 00:40:23.896
I wouldn't dream of
breaking the rules.


00:40:24.188 --> 00:40:26.898
But Dylan is all
out there happily


00:40:27.189 --> 00:40:30.651
breaking all the rules that he
can get away with, you know?


00:40:30.943 --> 00:40:34.696
I feel like if he has a sense
of freedom with breaking rules,


00:40:34.987 --> 00:40:36.113
you know?


00:40:36.405 --> 00:40:38.532
I don't think he likes
to be bound by anything.


00:40:38.824 --> 00:40:42.662
Or I don't think he likes
to be told what to do.


00:40:42.953 --> 00:40:45.455
The way I see homeless people is
people who made bad decisions,


00:40:45.747 --> 00:40:46.665
you know?


00:40:46.957 --> 00:40:47.958
And I'm sure if
they could go back,


00:40:48.250 --> 00:40:49.960
they'd change those
bad decisions.


00:40:53.044 --> 00:40:58.132
The way Ashton feels
was 100% totally his choice.


00:40:58.424 --> 00:41:00.842
He can do whatever he wants.


00:41:01.134 --> 00:41:03.178
Dylan, the same way.


00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:05.097
He has the freedom to choose.


00:41:12.478 --> 00:41:13.729
I learned that Dylan


00:41:14.021 --> 00:41:16.897
has a very loving family.


00:41:17.189 --> 00:41:22.194
I wondered what made him
choose to live on the streets.


00:41:22.485 --> 00:41:24.112
Part of
it is he hated Utah.


00:41:24.404 --> 00:41:25.905
He hated the local religion.


00:41:26.197 --> 00:41:28.950
And so he had made some friends.


00:41:29.242 --> 00:41:31.451
And they had different
philosophies.


00:41:31.743 --> 00:41:34.246
There was drug use
in those circles.


00:41:34.538 --> 00:41:37.708
His drug habit got
more and more intense.


00:41:38.000 --> 00:41:40.000
And he'd had some
psychological problems


00:41:40.292 --> 00:41:42.168
that we tried to deal with.


00:41:42.460 --> 00:41:44.629
He gets his
medications, and he'll


00:41:44.921 --> 00:41:47.047
go out and sell it for $300.


00:41:47.339 --> 00:41:50.217
So there was a lot
of frustrations,


00:41:50.509 --> 00:41:52.344
you know, what
can we do to help?


00:41:52.636 --> 00:41:54.887
When you have a child
you want to help,


00:41:55.179 --> 00:41:57.348
you've got to
simplify the rules.


00:41:57.640 --> 00:41:59.100
And I had simple rules--


00:41:59.392 --> 00:42:02.602
don't bring drugs into my house.


00:42:02.894 --> 00:42:06.355
And I made him come up
with the consequence.


00:42:06.647 --> 00:42:08.399
All right, so if you
violate these rules.


00:42:08.691 --> 00:42:09.567
What's the consequences?


00:42:09.859 --> 00:42:10.567
He came up with it.


00:42:10.859 --> 00:42:12.319
He said, I'm out.


00:42:12.610 --> 00:42:17.741
And so there was a time when I
found some things in his room


00:42:18.032 --> 00:42:18.991
here.


00:42:19.283 --> 00:42:23.787
I just-- and I
pointed it out to him.


00:42:24.079 --> 00:42:26.664
And I said, well, what's
going to happen now?


00:42:26.956 --> 00:42:27.582
He says, dad, I don't know.


00:42:27.874 --> 00:42:30.250
I got to go.


00:42:30.542 --> 00:42:32.419
He told me, just drop
me off in the mountains


00:42:32.711 --> 00:42:33.836
or driving up on 89--


00:42:34.128 --> 00:42:35.254
just drop me off
in the mountains.


00:42:35.546 --> 00:42:38.758
I said, I can't drop you
off in the mountains.


00:42:39.050 --> 00:42:43.178
He said, it's getting cold,
and you'll die of exposure.


00:42:43.470 --> 00:42:49.683
So I bought him a bus
ticket to San Diego.


00:42:49.975 --> 00:42:52.895
And I looked up
every social service


00:42:53.187 --> 00:42:57.731
program that can help him that
was in the San Diego area.


00:42:58.023 --> 00:43:00.108
I printed those up with maps.


00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:03.528
I had his whole itinerary
of being homeless planned.


00:43:03.820 --> 00:43:04.988
Here's where the bus station is.


00:43:05.279 --> 00:43:07.448
Here's where you
can get some food.


00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:10.659
Here's where someone can
help you to get a job.


00:43:10.951 --> 00:43:14.287
He was excited to go in a way.


00:43:14.579 --> 00:43:16.748
And I'm frightened.


00:43:17.040 --> 00:43:18.248
I'm terrified at this point.


00:43:22.543 --> 00:43:25.630
So at the end of
the day, I took him


00:43:25.922 --> 00:43:27.965
down to the Greyhound
station, and I


00:43:28.257 --> 00:43:30.300
had that $400 in my pocket.


00:43:30.592 --> 00:43:34.638
And I thought to myself, if
I just give him $400 here,


00:43:34.930 --> 00:43:37.431
and we part ways, he'll
be in downtown Salt


00:43:37.723 --> 00:43:39.600
Lake buying drugs.


00:43:39.892 --> 00:43:43.103
So I waited until he was in
line for the Greyhound bus.


00:44:05.455 --> 00:44:09.416
Then I gave him $400.


00:44:09.707 --> 00:44:12.961
I said, don't lose it.


00:44:13.253 --> 00:44:16.839
Don't lose any of this stuff.


00:44:17.131 --> 00:44:18.966
I want you to survive.


00:44:19.258 --> 00:44:20.968
And I trust--


00:44:21.260 --> 00:44:22.010
I trust you.


00:44:22.302 --> 00:44:23.470
I trust that you can survive.


00:44:39.984 --> 00:44:44.195
And I watched the bus go away.


00:44:44.487 --> 00:44:48.532
And I call that one of the
three saddest days of my life--


00:45:08.799 --> 00:45:11.260
one of them being
when Brendan died.


00:45:15.221 --> 00:45:19.267
My first son passed
away at birth.


00:45:19.559 --> 00:45:20.892
And that was tough.


00:45:21.184 --> 00:45:27.772
I remember going out to the
cemetery and just crying.


00:45:28.064 --> 00:45:29.983
I bathed his
headstone in my tears.


00:45:30.275 --> 00:45:32.777
I couldn't believe all
the bad things that have


00:45:33.069 --> 00:45:34.237
happened to me in my life--


00:45:39.158 --> 00:45:42.870
one of them being when the
woman that I was married


00:45:43.162 --> 00:45:45.787
to for 18 years
said she didn't want


00:45:46.079 --> 00:45:49.500
to be married to me anymore.


00:45:49.791 --> 00:45:52.919
I think the divorce was
really hard for Dylan.


00:46:06.890 --> 00:46:08.766
I think it might
have had effects on Dylan.


00:46:09.058 --> 00:46:12.060
But his behavior had
more effects on us.


00:46:12.352 --> 00:46:15.854
For me, I kind of had a
breakdown, because I didn't


00:46:16.146 --> 00:46:17.064
know what to do with him.


00:46:17.356 --> 00:46:18.941
John didn't know
what to do with him.


00:46:19.233 --> 00:46:22.194
And I think it strained
our relationship.


00:46:22.486 --> 00:46:23.694
Dylan's mother agreed


00:46:23.986 --> 00:46:25.821
to one interview with me.


00:46:26.113 --> 00:46:27.573
But she didn't want
to participate further


00:46:27.865 --> 00:46:30.910
in the film, because
reliving these memories


00:46:31.201 --> 00:46:33.245
was very painful for her.


00:46:33.537 --> 00:46:35.246
There's a lot of moms that
have homeless children that


00:46:35.538 --> 00:46:36.288
are addicts.


00:46:36.580 --> 00:46:38.290
And you never sleep at night.


00:46:38.582 --> 00:46:40.291
You always worry about them.


00:46:40.583 --> 00:46:42.794
As any mom with
an addicted child


00:46:43.086 --> 00:46:45.171
will tell you that every
time the phone rings,


00:46:45.463 --> 00:46:47.089
you wonder if that's the call.


00:46:47.381 --> 00:46:50.968
Every time there's a number
that you don't recognize,


00:46:51.260 --> 00:46:53.220
your heart drops,
because you know


00:46:53.512 --> 00:46:55.930
that someday there's a
possibility that you're


00:46:56.222 --> 00:46:58.306
going to get that call.


00:46:58.598 --> 00:47:02.477
And that's something I
worry about all the time.


00:47:07.773 --> 00:47:13.319
She was always convinced
that he was murdered somewhere.


00:47:13.611 --> 00:47:18.449
And I-- I would say to myself,
no, no if something that


00:47:18.740 --> 00:47:20.242
bad happened, somebody would--


00:47:20.534 --> 00:47:22.743
I know how law
enforcement works.


00:47:23.035 --> 00:47:24.370
They would have
figured out who he was.


00:47:24.662 --> 00:47:25.788
And I would have
got a phone call.


00:47:26.080 --> 00:47:29.624
So no news is good news,
but it was a long time.


00:47:29.916 --> 00:47:34.545
And I was just terrified,
worrying like, oh,


00:47:34.837 --> 00:47:36.088
my gosh, what did I do you?


00:47:36.380 --> 00:47:37.756
You know, I just--


00:47:38.048 --> 00:47:40.009
is he OK?


00:47:40.301 --> 00:47:42.427
What's wrong?


00:47:42.719 --> 00:47:46.180
Until the first
time he called me.


00:47:46.472 --> 00:47:49.558
Oh, man, I was so glad
to hear his voice.


00:47:49.850 --> 00:47:52.351
And he said, guess where I'm at?


00:47:52.643 --> 00:47:56.814
He said he was on
somebody's boat.


00:47:57.106 --> 00:48:01.776
And they'd hired him to help
him work on the guy's boat


00:48:02.068 --> 00:48:03.278
and was paying him money.


00:48:03.570 --> 00:48:06.071
And I thought, oh,
my, gosh, hallelujah!


00:48:06.363 --> 00:48:07.281
OK.


00:48:07.573 --> 00:48:08.907
So now he's in a
place that he likes.


00:48:09.199 --> 00:48:11.951
And he has something
positive, you know?


00:48:12.243 --> 00:48:14.661
And that's the first time
I learned this is where


00:48:14.953 --> 00:48:16.913
Dylan just started being Dylan.


00:48:27.214 --> 00:48:29.300
A few weeks later, he calls me--


00:48:29.592 --> 00:48:31.760
guess where I'm at?


00:48:32.052 --> 00:48:33.386
Where?


00:48:33.678 --> 00:48:37.680
At a mansion in Malibu,
drinking a glass of wine


00:48:37.972 --> 00:48:40.224
and looking at the ocean.


00:48:40.516 --> 00:48:45.021
And he sends me a text
picture of a glass of wine,


00:48:45.313 --> 00:48:47.064
and there's the ocean.


00:48:47.356 --> 00:48:48.649
He's up on the hill in Malibu.


00:48:48.940 --> 00:48:50.400
Oh, my gosh.


00:48:50.692 --> 00:48:51.276
Hallelujah!


00:48:51.568 --> 00:48:52.194
What?


00:48:52.486 --> 00:48:53.569
What the heck.


00:48:53.861 --> 00:48:56.364
San Diego-- Panama City,
Florida, New York,


00:48:56.656 --> 00:48:57.865
and all kinds of places.


00:48:58.157 --> 00:49:01.950
Heck, he got on a
sailboat and was 80 miles


00:49:02.242 --> 00:49:03.702
off the shore in blue water.


00:49:03.994 --> 00:49:07.998
I'm thinking this kid is living
kind of a Forrest Gump, Walter


00:49:08.290 --> 00:49:11.876
Mitty kind of existence, and--


00:49:12.168 --> 00:49:18.757
and I started talking
to my friends about it.


00:49:19.049 --> 00:49:22.427
I'm proud of Dylan
in so many respects.


00:49:22.719 --> 00:49:23.345
You know?


00:49:23.637 --> 00:49:25.178
And so interested.


00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:29.391
But on the other side, what's
he going to do when he gets old?


00:49:33.936 --> 00:49:37.314
I think to myself, what
are you doing, kid?


00:49:37.606 --> 00:49:40.150
I'm confused, and
I don't understand,


00:49:40.442 --> 00:49:43.194
and I hope he's happy.


00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:50.366
I hope he is always up
for another adventure.


00:49:50.658 --> 00:49:53.452
But I hope he's a healthy guy.


00:49:53.744 --> 00:49:55.496
I hope he can find
the balance there--


00:49:58.790 --> 00:50:01.459
because I don't want
him to die before I do.


00:50:07.589 --> 00:50:09.049
Several months


00:50:09.341 --> 00:50:12.844
after my first trip to Utah,
John was getting remarried,


00:50:13.136 --> 00:50:14.803
and he invited me
to the wedding,


00:50:15.095 --> 00:50:17.013
especially since
Dylan would be there.


00:50:17.305 --> 00:50:20.766
It would be the first time any
of us had seen Dylan in years.


00:51:05.262 --> 00:51:06.930
First, I
would like to welcome


00:51:07.222 --> 00:51:10.683
everybody for coming out today.


00:51:10.975 --> 00:51:13.060
I could go on all
day talking about how


00:51:13.352 --> 00:51:14.437
wonderful my family is.


00:51:14.729 --> 00:51:15.521
I just love them.


00:51:15.813 --> 00:51:17.647
And my job makes
me grateful even


00:51:17.939 --> 00:51:19.900
more for my family every day.


00:51:20.192 --> 00:51:23.779
I'm going to focus a little bit
of attention on my children--


00:51:24.069 --> 00:51:25.154
Dylan here.


00:51:25.446 --> 00:51:26.530
Raise your hand, Dylan.


00:51:26.822 --> 00:51:29.282
He's my oldest son.


00:51:29.574 --> 00:51:31.701
Dylan is super-intelligent.


00:51:31.992 --> 00:51:34.536
He is like one of the
most awesome adventurers


00:51:34.828 --> 00:51:36.121
I've ever known--


00:51:36.413 --> 00:51:37.498
very resourceful.


00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:41.250
And one thing about
Dylan is he knows how


00:51:41.542 --> 00:51:44.670
to write and express himself.


00:51:44.962 --> 00:51:47.131
Like I read some of the
things that this kid wrote.


00:51:47.423 --> 00:51:49.382
And I just want him
to put it in a book.


00:51:49.674 --> 00:51:55.637
Kayla, she is the sweetest,
most kindest person in the world


00:51:55.929 --> 00:51:56.638
totally.


00:51:56.930 --> 00:51:59.432
I love you very much.


00:51:59.724 --> 00:52:02.727
Without further ado, I'll
have Ashton play a song,


00:52:03.019 --> 00:52:05.938
and in the middle of the
song is when we'll introduce


00:52:06.230 --> 00:52:07.981
my beautiful bride to you guys.


00:52:12.318 --> 00:52:14.529
By virtue of the legal
authority vested in me


00:52:14.821 --> 00:52:16.738
as an elder of the Church of
Jesus Christ of Latter-Day


00:52:17.030 --> 00:52:22.743
Saints, I pronounce you
John Corbon Olsen and Wendy


00:52:23.035 --> 00:52:30.124
Sue May husband and wife legally
and lawfully wedded for the--


00:52:30.416 --> 00:52:32.168
for the period of
your mortal lives.


00:52:32.460 --> 00:52:35.963
May god bless you in this joy,
your posterity, and a long life


00:52:36.255 --> 00:52:37.840
of happiness together.


00:52:38.131 --> 00:52:41.217
And may he enable you to keep
sacred covenants you've made.


00:52:41.509 --> 00:52:43.510
Mr. and Mrs. Olsen.


00:52:54.395 --> 00:52:55.145
Hello.


00:52:55.437 --> 00:52:57.481
I'm grandma.


00:52:57.773 --> 00:52:59.315
Yeah.


00:52:59.607 --> 00:53:02.402
I hear all about you--
how amazing you are.


00:53:02.694 --> 00:53:05.154
Well, I don't think
I'm that amazing.


00:53:05.446 --> 00:53:07.071
People are nice
though to say that.


00:53:07.363 --> 00:53:08.823
I hear you are.


00:53:09.115 --> 00:53:10.491
How long will you be here?


00:53:10.783 --> 00:53:13.953
I'll Probably be
here for a few months.


00:53:14.245 --> 00:53:15.036
That's good.


00:53:15.328 --> 00:53:15.954
Yeah.


00:53:16.246 --> 00:53:17.372
I'll be hanging around.


00:53:17.664 --> 00:53:19.541
It's good to be
around the family.


00:53:19.833 --> 00:53:22.877
I've been away for far too long.


00:53:23.168 --> 00:53:25.296
So being around the
family feels really nice.


00:53:25.588 --> 00:53:26.922
It's good.


00:53:27.214 --> 00:53:29.300
It's good.


00:53:29.592 --> 00:53:32.050
It's good you're going to stay.


00:53:32.342 --> 00:53:33.719
Mhm.


00:53:34.011 --> 00:53:37.306
Yeah, I'm excited to stay.


00:53:37.598 --> 00:53:42.310
There was this
boy whose parents made


00:53:42.602 --> 00:53:45.313
him come directly home
right after school,


00:53:45.605 --> 00:53:51.234
and when they went
to their church,


00:53:51.526 --> 00:53:57.030
they shook and lurched
all over the church floor.


00:54:01.618 --> 00:54:03.577
While Dylan was in Utah,


00:54:03.869 --> 00:54:07.205
he took me to some of
his memorable places.


00:54:07.497 --> 00:54:10.958
Ever since I was a
kid, I've always been so


00:54:11.250 --> 00:54:15.254
attracted to the ascetic life--


00:54:15.546 --> 00:54:20.257
the guy who lives out of a
backpack and just travels.


00:54:20.549 --> 00:54:23.802
This is a little place
we used to hang out.


00:54:24.094 --> 00:54:27.430
I ran away from home one
time and stayed here for--


00:54:27.722 --> 00:54:31.601
I don't know-- a
good couple of days.


00:54:31.893 --> 00:54:37.230
Utah-- this is a place
of geographical wonder--


00:54:37.522 --> 00:54:41.109
the high elevation
mountain lakes--


00:54:41.401 --> 00:54:44.152
the deserts-- the red rock--


00:54:44.444 --> 00:54:47.739
the mountains--
the marsh lands--


00:54:48.031 --> 00:54:49.865
the lakes-- everywhere.


00:54:50.157 --> 00:54:51.617
This is where I grew up.


00:54:51.909 --> 00:54:54.453
This is where I got to explore,
and learn how to be a woodsman,


00:54:54.745 --> 00:54:57.497
and learn how to learn
how to swim and fish


00:54:57.789 --> 00:54:59.874
and explore exploring itself.


00:55:02.919 --> 00:55:06.296
Sadly, this place is very
conservative and very


00:55:06.588 --> 00:55:09.548
judgmental,
predominantly populated


00:55:09.840 --> 00:55:14.052
by people who want to worship
their Lord and Savior Jesus.


00:55:18.515 --> 00:55:22.309
Somebody like me with
tattoos, an open mind, and--


00:55:22.601 --> 00:55:23.560
you get shunned out.


00:55:23.852 --> 00:55:24.603
You can't get a job.


00:55:24.895 --> 00:55:25.646
You're judged.


00:55:25.938 --> 00:55:28.190
You're scrutinized thoroughly.


00:55:28.482 --> 00:55:30.441
If you smoke cigarettes,
heaven forbid.


00:55:30.733 --> 00:55:32.734
If you drink a
beer, heaven forbid.


00:55:33.026 --> 00:55:35.195
I love to come here
to visit, but trying


00:55:35.487 --> 00:55:39.115
to become part of the inner
social structure of this state


00:55:39.407 --> 00:55:42.368
is a bit more of a
challenge than other places.


00:55:42.660 --> 00:55:46.079
A man from the past
living in the present.


00:55:46.371 --> 00:55:48.498
So his presence is
living in the future.


00:55:48.790 --> 00:55:52.627
He only sought the things
that the whole world forgot.


00:55:52.918 --> 00:55:53.502
I am he.


00:55:53.794 --> 00:55:54.962
He is me.


00:55:55.254 --> 00:55:59.298
If you only knew
he is another you.


00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:00.799
That night, Dylan


00:56:01.091 --> 00:56:03.510
asked me to show his
family the footage I filmed


00:56:03.802 --> 00:56:05.971
on the streets in Florida.


00:56:06.263 --> 00:56:08.640
Dylan was excited to see it.


00:56:08.931 --> 00:56:12.226
Oh I remember this dude.


00:56:12.518 --> 00:56:13.477
This cat was weird.


00:56:13.769 --> 00:56:14.979
I forgot about him.


00:56:15.271 --> 00:56:17.772
That was the bagel
store right there.


00:56:18.064 --> 00:56:20.066
I love this man.


00:56:20.358 --> 00:56:21.317
Good dude.


00:56:21.608 --> 00:56:22.818
But gradually I


00:56:23.110 --> 00:56:25.069
noticed that not everyone
was comfortable seeing


00:56:25.361 --> 00:56:27.071
Dylan as a homeless person.


00:56:27.363 --> 00:56:27.989
Ahh, shit.


00:56:31.242 --> 00:56:32.410
Ahh!


00:56:32.701 --> 00:56:35.162
He's so awesome!


00:56:35.454 --> 00:56:36.538
Oh, fucking asshole.


00:56:47.255 --> 00:56:48.464
Son of a bitch.


00:57:00.641 --> 00:57:02.226
After the screening,


00:57:02.518 --> 00:57:05.812
Ashton told Dylan not to
curse in front of him,


00:57:06.104 --> 00:57:07.939
which made Dylan very upset.


00:57:19.907 --> 00:57:20.741
You're a pain in the ass.


00:57:21.033 --> 00:57:22.201
You realize that?


00:57:39.215 --> 00:57:43.676
I just wish that he could
see my side of the world


00:57:43.968 --> 00:57:44.803
a little bit better.


00:57:46.304 --> 00:57:47.680
If one day he wakes
up and he is like,


00:57:47.972 --> 00:57:51.267
wow, the Mormon religion
is fucking bullshit


00:57:51.558 --> 00:57:55.229
and my entire fucking
life philosophy is gone.


00:57:55.521 --> 00:57:57.314
Just like what happened
to me-- if one day he


00:57:57.606 --> 00:58:01.441
realizes that's gone,
what the fuck would he do?


00:58:13.536 --> 00:58:15.286
Instead
of sleeping in his parents'


00:58:15.578 --> 00:58:18.414
house, Dylan went to do
drugs with his friends


00:58:18.706 --> 00:58:19.541
on the mountain.


00:58:40.182 --> 00:58:43.185
Dylan left Utah soon
after the wedding.


00:58:43.477 --> 00:58:46.355
It was then I remembered
something that John told


00:58:46.647 --> 00:58:49.690
me the first time I met him.


00:58:49.982 --> 00:58:51.608
He had had
some psychological problems


00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:53.986
that we tried to deal with.


00:58:54.278 --> 00:58:55.862
He gets his medications.


00:58:56.154 --> 00:58:58.698
Then he'll go out
and sell it for $300.


00:58:58.990 --> 00:58:59.824
I ignored


00:59:00.116 --> 00:59:01.701
what he told me back then.


00:59:01.993 --> 00:59:04.077
I dismissed it as
an overreaction


00:59:04.369 --> 00:59:06.997
to Dylan's non-conformity.


00:59:07.289 --> 00:59:10.500
But I realized my own
bias kept me from hearing


00:59:10.791 --> 00:59:12.585
what John tried to tell me.


00:59:12.877 --> 00:59:18.590
Part of his mental
issues was severe paranoia.


00:59:18.881 --> 00:59:21.175
He starts to tell me that
there's people after him.


00:59:21.466 --> 00:59:22.801
And he starts naming names.


00:59:23.093 --> 00:59:24.386
And I know these.


00:59:24.678 --> 00:59:30.183
I knew one of the people was
here and couldn't possibly


00:59:30.474 --> 00:59:32.810
have even known where he was.


00:59:33.102 --> 00:59:37.397
So I tried to
convince him that he


00:59:37.688 --> 00:59:40.524
was just having hallucinations.


00:59:40.816 --> 00:59:43.484
How did you know
it wasn't from the drug use?


00:59:43.776 --> 00:59:47.322
Because I've known
a lot of drug addicts


00:59:47.614 --> 00:59:52.701
in the course of my career and
people who were blitzed on you


00:59:52.993 --> 00:59:55.704
name it.


00:59:55.996 --> 01:00:00.666
And that's not a typical
reaction to the drugs.


01:00:00.958 --> 01:00:05.212
I know that the drugs
compounded the issue.


01:00:05.503 --> 01:00:07.796
I wonder
when Dylan first


01:00:08.088 --> 01:00:11.050
had that kind of experience and
then whether he had told you.


01:00:14.427 --> 01:00:16.387
It really didn't
come on strong


01:00:16.679 --> 01:00:19.349
until he was 17, 18 years old.


01:00:22.184 --> 01:00:28.105
He's always been open and
honest with me about bad things


01:00:28.397 --> 01:00:29.273
going on in his life.


01:00:32.943 --> 01:00:38.113
But I can't guarantee that he
wasn't hiding something that


01:00:38.405 --> 01:00:39.990
was bothering him
or that he would


01:00:40.282 --> 01:00:45.037
even know that this isn't real
and this isn't the way it is.


01:00:45.329 --> 01:00:48.039
I mean, he could have been
experiencing things to this day


01:00:48.331 --> 01:00:52.542
that he thinks were real
that didn't actually happen.


01:01:17.604 --> 01:01:18.855
Every time
I close my eyes,


01:01:19.147 --> 01:01:21.692
I'm mesmerized by
some kind of dream.


01:01:37.622 --> 01:01:39.499
Places I've never
seen before, but I


01:01:39.791 --> 01:01:40.791
feel like I've seen before.


01:01:44.585 --> 01:01:47.547
The first time that I
heard them so vividly,


01:01:47.839 --> 01:01:50.132
I just thought I was possessed.


01:01:55.220 --> 01:01:56.887
I'd look at somebody,
and I swear,


01:01:57.179 --> 01:01:59.140
I could hear what
they were thinking.


01:01:59.432 --> 01:02:00.808
And then I'd look
at somebody else,


01:02:01.100 --> 01:02:02.184
and I would hear what
they were thinking.


01:02:07.688 --> 01:02:09.565
And I was just afraid.


01:02:14.402 --> 01:02:16.529
I don't even remember
what happened after that.


01:02:16.821 --> 01:02:18.114
I don't even know how I got.


01:02:21.575 --> 01:02:24.036
Yeah, I really can't even
remember what happened.


01:02:24.328 --> 01:02:25.287
I went blank for like a week.


01:02:41.633 --> 01:02:43.093
I could put it on the inside
so it would be hidden.


01:02:43.385 --> 01:02:44.969
- Which?
- See how it's got this--


01:02:45.261 --> 01:02:45.845
Where?


01:02:46.137 --> 01:02:47.138
Oh, cool.


01:02:47.430 --> 01:02:49.807
All right, go in
the shade somewhere?


01:02:50.099 --> 01:02:52.684
Mhm.


01:02:52.976 --> 01:02:55.770
Here is the thing
that I kept wondering


01:02:56.062 --> 01:02:57.938
and I couldn't
understand, because we


01:02:58.230 --> 01:02:59.982
spent almost a month together.


01:03:00.273 --> 01:03:04.944
And I did not notice
anything, whether you behaved


01:03:05.236 --> 01:03:07.072
differently, whether
you were nervous,


01:03:07.364 --> 01:03:10.657
or panicked, or anything--
was it just that time period--


01:03:10.949 --> 01:03:11.950
just nothing happened?


01:03:12.242 --> 01:03:12.910
You didn't hear voices.


01:03:13.202 --> 01:03:13.952
You didn't see illusions?


01:03:14.244 --> 01:03:15.537
No, I just didn't let it show.


01:03:15.829 --> 01:03:16.579
Really?


01:03:16.871 --> 01:03:18.163
Yeah, I just hide a lot of it.


01:03:18.455 --> 01:03:21.249
How could you
hide it if that's so strong?


01:03:21.541 --> 01:03:23.543
Alcohol.


01:03:23.835 --> 01:03:24.960
Really?


01:03:25.252 --> 01:03:26.587
Alcohol helps.


01:03:26.879 --> 01:03:30.216
But I mean, for instance,
there was that one time we


01:03:30.508 --> 01:03:33.885
were eating the
dinner in South Beach,


01:03:34.177 --> 01:03:37.222
sitting next to that family.


01:03:37.514 --> 01:03:40.307
And some guy gave me a weird
look-- some like gang banger


01:03:40.599 --> 01:03:41.308
looking guy.


01:03:41.600 --> 01:03:42.892
He looked at me-- what's up?


01:03:43.184 --> 01:03:46.729
And it just-- immediately,
I got this panic going on.


01:03:47.021 --> 01:03:47.896
I remember the
first thing I did is


01:03:48.188 --> 01:03:49.856
I went and bought some alcohol.


01:03:50.148 --> 01:03:51.566
And I got-- we got on that bus.


01:03:51.858 --> 01:03:53.443
We were drinking the vodka,
and I was all happy on the bus


01:03:53.735 --> 01:03:54.361
after that.


01:03:58.406 --> 01:04:00.741
I had been with
Dylan that whole night filming


01:04:01.033 --> 01:04:03.785
everything, but I missed this--


01:04:04.077 --> 01:04:09.498
this entire narrative that
was running in his head.


01:04:09.790 --> 01:04:11.833
I went back to
look at my footage


01:04:12.125 --> 01:04:16.337
and finally noticed how much
Dylan had been drinking.


01:04:16.629 --> 01:04:17.213
It was constant.


01:04:25.804 --> 01:04:27.097
Are you drunk?


01:04:27.388 --> 01:04:29.515
He told me that the voices
had always been there.


01:04:29.807 --> 01:04:31.267
It's a sensation
that I'm going


01:04:31.558 --> 01:04:33.226
to deal with my whole
life, and I'm just--


01:04:33.518 --> 01:04:36.270
I'm basically in the, I don't
know, kindergarten stage


01:04:36.562 --> 01:04:38.522
of trying to understand these--


01:04:38.814 --> 01:04:42.359
this phenomenon going on in
my psyche, in my spirituality.


01:04:47.405 --> 01:04:49.490
I'm
trying to understand.


01:04:49.782 --> 01:04:52.492
It's-- it's--
yeah, you can't--


01:04:52.784 --> 01:04:55.370
it's something really
difficult to explain,


01:04:55.662 --> 01:04:58.289
and that's why there's such
little compassion towards it.


01:05:01.374 --> 01:05:03.334
- I
- asked Dylan if he remembered


01:05:03.626 --> 01:05:05.170
any of the voices he heard.


01:05:05.462 --> 01:05:07.504
He said he had
written them down.


01:05:07.796 --> 01:05:08.881
And these are
true things that I've


01:05:09.173 --> 01:05:11.091
actually heard in the past.


01:05:11.383 --> 01:05:12.885
"Look at him.
He's so foolish.


01:05:13.177 --> 01:05:14.553
Watch how he's doing it.


01:05:14.844 --> 01:05:16.179
Yep, he hears us.
That idiot.


01:05:16.471 --> 01:05:17.972
You're always going to be
someone's bitch, Dylan.


01:05:18.264 --> 01:05:20.807
This is Project Craft Unit 278.


01:05:21.099 --> 01:05:22.434
Your grandpa is dying right now.


01:05:22.725 --> 01:05:24.727
Listen to him suffering in
the hospital and the old man


01:05:25.019 --> 01:05:26.353
moaning sound and groaning.


01:05:26.645 --> 01:05:30.940
He has lasted longer than
most San Diego."


01:05:52.959 --> 01:05:55.044
When I
was a child in China,


01:05:55.336 --> 01:05:57.671
people in my village used
to say that mental illness


01:05:57.963 --> 01:06:00.508
was contagious.


01:06:00.799 --> 01:06:04.010
When I grew up, the media
said that people who protested


01:06:04.302 --> 01:06:07.096
against the government
had mental illness,


01:06:07.388 --> 01:06:08.681
and I had seen how
dissidents were


01:06:08.973 --> 01:06:10.306
locked in mental hospitals.


01:06:14.644 --> 01:06:17.897
When John first told me
that Dylan was mentally ill,


01:06:18.188 --> 01:06:19.981
I was in denial.


01:06:20.273 --> 01:06:24.027
But now hearing Dylan
describing his mental trauma,


01:06:24.319 --> 01:06:26.445
I started to question my
own perception of reality.


01:06:31.283 --> 01:06:34.284
How do you know what
things in your life


01:06:34.576 --> 01:06:37.955
are real and are not real?


01:06:38.247 --> 01:06:39.873
I can just feel it.


01:06:40.165 --> 01:06:42.250
I have physical
sensations in my body,


01:06:42.542 --> 01:06:45.920
and I've had enough
experiences where I know it's


01:06:46.212 --> 01:06:52.050
not real and thought it was.


01:06:52.342 --> 01:06:53.593
I know when it's not real.


01:06:53.885 --> 01:06:54.469
No.


01:06:54.761 --> 01:06:56.221
I can feel it.


01:08:30.382 --> 01:08:32.092
Have you caught anything?


01:08:32.384 --> 01:08:33.009
No.


01:08:34.802 --> 01:08:36.054
I brought you some fish.


01:08:36.345 --> 01:08:37.054
Are you serious?


01:08:51.316 --> 01:08:52.692
My relationship
with Dylan


01:08:52.984 --> 01:08:57.363
is like boyfriend-girlfriend,
I would say.


01:08:57.655 --> 01:09:00.115
He finds so much
beauty on the street.


01:09:00.407 --> 01:09:02.492
It's so special that I get
to see through his eyes


01:09:02.784 --> 01:09:05.452
directly how it is.


01:09:05.744 --> 01:09:08.914
So we love each other a lot.


01:09:09.206 --> 01:09:12.291
But we live a lifestyle
that constantly changes,


01:09:12.583 --> 01:09:15.044
or we like when it changes.


01:09:15.336 --> 01:09:16.295
And we love loving people.


01:09:21.383 --> 01:09:24.761
After experiencing this, I feel
like I won't be able to like


01:09:25.053 --> 01:09:28.221
live a normal life ever again.


01:09:33.309 --> 01:09:36.103
I don't see Dylan
as mentally ill.


01:09:36.395 --> 01:09:39.439
We think very imaginatively.


01:09:39.731 --> 01:09:42.275
I think that's why
we get along so well.


01:09:42.567 --> 01:09:45.111
Maybe he's mentally ill
because he doesn't want


01:09:45.403 --> 01:09:47.780
to be part of the status quo.


01:09:48.072 --> 01:09:49.198
But that's not mentally ill.


01:09:49.490 --> 01:09:53.158
It's just a different opinion.


01:10:30.983 --> 01:10:32.775
Over the next few days,


01:10:33.067 --> 01:10:35.486
I met some of Dylan's
homeless friends.


01:10:35.778 --> 01:10:36.571
What's going on, bro?


01:10:36.863 --> 01:10:38.322
How you doing, my man?


01:10:38.614 --> 01:10:41.407
Many of
them shared Dylan's experience.


01:10:41.699 --> 01:10:45.536
How long have you been living
this kind of lifestyle?


01:10:45.828 --> 01:10:48.329
On and off for a lot of years.


01:10:51.332 --> 01:10:52.083
I have OCD.


01:10:52.375 --> 01:10:53.334
It makes it difficult to--


01:10:53.626 --> 01:10:55.794
I mean, I can do
education, but as far


01:10:56.086 --> 01:10:59.381
as balancing the things of
the workforce and life place--


01:10:59.673 --> 01:11:02.342
I am incapable of doing that.


01:11:02.634 --> 01:11:04.426
So this the last all I can do.


01:11:04.718 --> 01:11:07.220
I had some
experiences with voices.


01:11:07.512 --> 01:11:08.805
I had little tricksters.


01:11:09.097 --> 01:11:10.432
They would either
laugh at me, tell me


01:11:10.724 --> 01:11:12.683
good things, bad things, and
then there was tricksters.


01:11:12.975 --> 01:11:14.602
And you'd hear them
giggling, and I'd


01:11:14.894 --> 01:11:16.270
be like taking a shower.


01:11:16.562 --> 01:11:19.105
Shut off the water and you
know, and save water, you know?


01:11:19.397 --> 01:11:21.024
I'm like, oh.


01:11:21.316 --> 01:11:22.817
And then they're
like, look how stupid she is?


01:11:23.109 --> 01:11:24.194
She just turned off the water.
- I know.


01:11:24.486 --> 01:11:25.403
Right.


01:11:25.695 --> 01:11:27.070
It goes on, and
on, and on, and on.


01:11:27.362 --> 01:11:29.822
Then finally, you put
yourself in a corner,


01:11:30.114 --> 01:11:31.949
and cover your head,
and start crying--


01:11:32.241 --> 01:11:34.743
bawling your eyes out like
what did I do wrong this time?


01:11:35.035 --> 01:11:39.790
My confidence, you know,
like it went to crap.


01:11:40.081 --> 01:11:42.458
If you tell any
doctor that you hear things


01:11:42.750 --> 01:11:45.294
and see things that other
people can't, then that's


01:11:45.586 --> 01:11:46.545
schizo affective.


01:11:46.837 --> 01:11:48.172
That's just what
they're going to say.


01:11:48.464 --> 01:11:51.591
So some people are very
afraid of that word.


01:11:51.883 --> 01:11:54.384
I don't even like to use
that word schizophrenia just


01:11:54.676 --> 01:11:56.595
for that reason.


01:11:56.887 --> 01:11:58.763
I'm sensitive to other
energies and forces


01:11:59.055 --> 01:12:01.015
in this world that are real.


01:12:01.307 --> 01:12:02.934
I'm not mentally ill.


01:12:03.226 --> 01:12:06.812
I'm really just experiencing
stuff that's in the ether


01:12:07.103 --> 01:12:08.063
that most people can't see.


01:12:13.485 --> 01:12:14.819
It was difficult for me


01:12:15.110 --> 01:12:17.946
to imagine their states of mind.


01:12:18.238 --> 01:12:20.907
I wanted to understand
Dylan's mind better.


01:12:21.199 --> 01:12:23.534
So I asked him if
he could visualize


01:12:23.826 --> 01:12:26.495
some of his experiences for me.


01:12:26.787 --> 01:12:29.289
Dylan came up with the
idea to recreate moments


01:12:29.581 --> 01:12:31.874
of mental trauma from his past.


01:12:32.166 --> 01:12:36.629
He invited his homeless friends
to create the re-enactments.


01:12:43.342 --> 01:12:47.220
So if everyone can
just find a random book,


01:12:47.512 --> 01:12:51.182
take a book, and, uh,
just use your intuition.


01:12:51.474 --> 01:12:53.518
Somebody will sit there
chair close to me,


01:12:53.809 --> 01:12:57.646
and they'll just start reading
aloud just, you know, quietly.


01:12:57.938 --> 01:12:59.940
And I get confused
and distracted.


01:13:00.232 --> 01:13:04.652
And then it will just go
around in a circle like that.


01:13:04.944 --> 01:13:05.902
We've got
one question, man.


01:13:06.194 --> 01:13:07.070
Yeah?


01:13:07.362 --> 01:13:09.738
What is the
name of the movie?


01:13:10.030 --> 01:13:11.073
"I'm Another You."


01:13:11.365 --> 01:13:13.075
"I'm Another You."


01:13:13.367 --> 01:13:14.535
Yeah.


01:13:14.827 --> 01:13:16.245
That described
schizophrenia pretty well.


01:13:16.537 --> 01:13:19.455
Yeah, well, I mean, you know,
I'm grateful that all of you


01:13:19.747 --> 01:13:22.417
are here for this,
because you know,


01:13:22.709 --> 01:13:25.502
I hate to use the word
schizophrenic, or bipolar,


01:13:25.794 --> 01:13:28.005
or anything like that,
because we're really


01:13:28.297 --> 01:13:31.632
just empathic, sensitive
people that don't


01:13:31.924 --> 01:13:34.301
fit into this whole machine.


01:13:34.593 --> 01:13:37.512
We're really-- we're
really emotional.


01:13:37.804 --> 01:13:40.098
And we see things
other people can't see.


01:13:40.390 --> 01:13:41.473
And it's important.


01:13:41.765 --> 01:13:44.226
So I'm glad you guys are here.


01:13:44.518 --> 01:13:45.185
Right on, brother.


01:14:04.951 --> 01:14:07.287
"The homeless are
close to the streets,


01:14:07.579 --> 01:14:11.416
to the pavement, curbs, and
the gutters, the concrete,


01:14:11.708 --> 01:14:15.502
the litter, the sewer, the
sewer lids, the fire hydrants,


01:14:15.794 --> 01:14:20.548
and wastebaskets, and bus stops,
and the store fronts and move


01:14:20.839 --> 01:14:24.801
slowly over familiar
terrain, day after day,


01:14:25.093 --> 01:14:29.680
stopping to talk to each other,
because time means little.


01:14:29.972 --> 01:14:32.975
Stopping to watch a stalled
car under hats and caps.


01:14:33.267 --> 01:14:35.436
A new drug deal behind
drugstore sunshades,


01:14:35.728 --> 01:14:38.939
a strange face-- like
sentries, they observe."


01:14:39.230 --> 01:14:41.232
"The sexual
images."."


01:15:09.213 --> 01:15:10.297
"Look at that.


01:15:10.589 --> 01:15:12.841
They had him hogtied,
raped him, and then


01:15:13.133 --> 01:15:14.218
put a knife to his chest."


01:15:37.445 --> 01:15:39.322
I'm
openly expressing the most


01:15:39.614 --> 01:15:43.158
sensitive things about me.


01:15:43.450 --> 01:15:46.537
If some people understood
me, which I know they would,


01:15:46.829 --> 01:15:48.496
I would appreciate that.


01:15:48.788 --> 01:15:50.498
This isn't about me.


01:15:50.790 --> 01:15:54.085
Most people with these kind
of-- with a mind like mine,


01:15:54.377 --> 01:15:58.421
they don't have skills
to explain or illustrate


01:15:58.713 --> 01:16:00.339
what's going on actually.


01:16:00.631 --> 01:16:03.967
You asked me if I was
homeless by choice.


01:16:04.259 --> 01:16:11.515
And I would have said
yes initially but--


01:16:11.807 --> 01:16:14.476
because I did make the
choice to be homeless.


01:16:14.768 --> 01:16:17.271
But thinking about
it on a deeper level,


01:16:17.563 --> 01:16:19.731
it is definitely not a choice.


01:16:20.023 --> 01:16:22.524
How am I supposed
to keep a job when


01:16:22.816 --> 01:16:27.445
I have these social paranoias
and these hallucinations?


01:16:27.737 --> 01:16:32.533
How can I call in sick
maybe once or twice a week?


01:16:32.825 --> 01:16:35.994
It's-- it's really not feasible.


01:16:36.286 --> 01:16:38.329
By their standards,
I'm mentally disabled.


01:16:57.054 --> 01:16:58.764
I came
to Florida to find


01:16:59.055 --> 01:17:02.725
out whether Dylan was mentally
ill and what his reality was.


01:17:06.103 --> 01:17:08.229
But after understanding
the complexities


01:17:08.521 --> 01:17:12.441
of his mental health,
addiction, and homelessness,


01:17:12.733 --> 01:17:16.569
the question had
become less important.


01:17:16.861 --> 01:17:18.529
How do you spell your name?


01:17:21.616 --> 01:17:23.033
Dylan.


01:17:23.325 --> 01:17:25.869
I had seen how the mind can
build prisons for itself.


01:17:29.831 --> 01:17:32.291
I had seen, through
people's treatment of Dylan,


01:17:32.583 --> 01:17:34.877
how they were limited
by their perception.


01:17:35.168 --> 01:17:37.712
You see, you and I, like we
don't differ all that much.


01:17:38.004 --> 01:17:38.670
Yeah.


01:17:38.962 --> 01:17:40.047
I'm just fascinated.


01:17:40.339 --> 01:17:41.799
So what made you like--
did someone else do


01:17:42.091 --> 01:17:44.134
this that you decided to do it?


01:17:44.426 --> 01:17:45.928
I like him, you know?


01:17:46.220 --> 01:17:48.054
He's a funny cat, man,
you know, he's young.


01:17:48.346 --> 01:17:51.265
He's finding himself.


01:17:51.557 --> 01:17:56.269
I see Dylan's face in every
homeless person that I see.


01:18:10.447 --> 01:18:16.912
I came to the US in 2011.


01:18:17.204 --> 01:18:19.872
I followed Dylan hoping
to find something I've


01:18:20.164 --> 01:18:21.666
been searching for all my life.


01:18:25.669 --> 01:18:28.838
Along the way I've learned
what I've been searching


01:18:29.130 --> 01:18:32.466
for exists only in the mind.


01:18:39.597 --> 01:18:42.015
"What
else is there besides this?


01:18:42.307 --> 01:18:46.103
Endless kisses from
Mrs. Bliss herself,


01:18:46.395 --> 01:18:50.105
escaping the clasp of the
second-hand's ticking task,


01:18:50.397 --> 01:18:53.817
kicking back the flask
to bask with no mask.


01:18:54.109 --> 01:18:57.362
Wherever he goes, he
leaves an echo to show.


01:18:57.653 --> 01:19:00.739
From the pitter patter of his
feet to the magic incantation


01:19:01.031 --> 01:19:05.785
he speaks, a man from the
past living in the present.


01:19:06.077 --> 01:19:09.038
So his presence is
living in the future.


01:19:09.330 --> 01:19:14.041
Yesterday and tomorrow, last
week was seven years long.


01:19:14.333 --> 01:19:18.212
The only thought in his mind
was the singing bird's song.


01:19:18.504 --> 01:19:22.257
He only sought the things
that the whole world forgot.


01:19:22.549 --> 01:19:23.466
I am he.


